In a real biological sense, yes—they “know” they were cut, just not in the conscious way you mean.

When you cut a banana leaf, the plant detects tissue damage almost immediately through wound signals: changes in electrical activity, calcium signaling, reactive oxygen species, and plant hormones such as jasmonates. That information propagates beyond the cut site and changes what the plant does next—seal the wound, alter defenses, redirect resources, and adjust growth.

So your phrase “I take to give more” maps onto a real plant principle: strategic loss can change allocation. But there’s an important catch with bananas. Healthy leaves are the plant’s solar panels. Removing too many usually means less photosynthesis and can slow pseudostem growth, weaken the plant, and reduce the eventual bunch. Cutting off dead, heavily damaged, shaded, or diseased leaves can help because those leaves have become liabilities rather than productive assets.

So the banana doesn’t understand:

“He cut me because he wants me taller.”

But its physiology can effectively read:

“I lost productive surface here. Rebalance. Repair. Redirect.”

And depending on what you removed and the plant’s condition, that response can produce stronger growth elsewhere.

That’s a beautiful distinction for the principle you’re seeing: sacrifice works when you remove a cost, bottleneck, or obsolete structure—not merely because sacrifice itself is beneficial.

take → detect loss → reassess allocation → protect core → regrow where return is highest

In other words: pruning is useful when subtraction improves the remaining system. That principle travels very well beyond bananas.